{"page_content": "Figure R.16-13: Fate processes in the air compartment\n\ne Agricultural use\n\nIn the agricultural use scenario the air compartment receives its input from volatilisation during spray application (when spray application takes place). For consistency with the current EUSES implementation, the emission to air is considered as a point source release. In particular, air concentration at the receptor is calculated 100 m away from the source.\n\nCompared with EUSES implementation a difference is that the air concentration during episode (i.e. spraying task) is not averaged over a year but over a 30 days period to get Clocalair due to the reduced emission days in a year. This is then added to the PECregional for the calculation of PEClocalair, which is used for the calculation of the intake of substances through inhalation in the indirect exposure of humans.\n\nMore detailed information on the model is provided in Appendix A.16-2.4.\n\nR.16-4.3.8 Predators (secondary poisoning)\n\nThe predicted concentration in the food for predators, i.e. the concentration in worms and fish, are estimated on the basis of:\n\n- For fish-eating predators: the local and regional PECs for surface water, the bioconcentration factor (BCF) for fish and biomagnification factors (BMF). The PEC is calculated as the mean of the concentration in the (top) predator food obtained from the PEC local and from the PEC regional. Top predators are considered for marine water assessment only. (see Appendix A.16-2.2 for more information on BCF and BMF and their roles in the calculations). To be noted that when assessing an agricultural use (i.e. a direct release, not via STP sludge), fish-eating predators and top predators are not considered, as it is assumed that the ditch is not connected to other surface water bodies (therefore, not connected to the marine environment).\n\n- For worm-eating predators: the local and regional PEC for soil and BCF for worms. The PEC is calculated as the mean of the concentration in the predator food obtained from the PEC\n\nlocal and from the PEC regional.\n\nIn this section, the general principles are outlined. The details of the individual assessment steps and PEC calculations are described in Appendix A.16-2.3.8.", "metadata": {"doc_id": "aa95e6ae-f309-494f-8cd0-d4a47452fde1", "content_type": "CompositeElement", "filename": "IR_CSR_R16_V4_FINAL.pdf"}}